ce of Quebec like I said  hot topic Abigail the Prime Minister  also talks about the need to transition  to green energy and to lower emissions  so how is the oil industry reacting to  what he says well Donna the Canadian  Association of Petroleum Producers tells  Global News they were happy with  Trudeau's answer they felt the oil  industry was supported but they also say  no one knows where that industry will be  in a year from now in a recent earnings  call the CFO of Shell said they don't  even know if demand devastated during  the pandemic will come back stay  the Canadian Association of petroleum  producer says that's still a far cry  from a dead industry you know I think  it's a foolish thing to say when every  single Canadian can look at their  personal circumstances on a daily basis  and how it's been touched by the moving  of transportation movement of goods the  food we eat all of it is reliant on oil  and gas but today Elizabeth may double  down on those comments writing a  magazine op-ed with the headline the  reports of oils death are not greatly  exaggerated Donna  all right Abigail Beeman in Ottawa thank  you  next fighting for the future of news  [Music]  when a major news event happens  Canadians expect journalists to be there  and during times of crisis like a  pandemic people turn to news  organizations they know and trust  ratings are up across the board right  now but ad revenue is plummeting  Kovach 19 has sped up a problem that's  been plaguing the industry especially  newspapers for years though as Eric  Sorenson explains two international tech  giant's are still reaping rewards at a  time when journalism is so vital many of  the biggest newspapers in the country  are imploring Ottawa to confront  Internet giant's Facebook and Google  newspapers are hurting in recent days  new cutbacks the Kingsville reporter and  more than a dozen other community papers  in Ontario and Manitoba are closing  major publishers who normally compete  have signed a joint letter stating they  are suffering huge advertising revenue  declines because of the corona virus  pandemic and are calling on Ottawa to  make Google and Facebook pay their fair  share  it's estimated 80% of digital  advertising worldwide flows to Google  and Facebook but they don't pay Canadian  news providers for their content the big  Internet companies have been getting a  free ride this executive for Global News  parent company says broadcasting is also  suffering while news ratings are up  advertising revenue is down the reality  is these internet companies who have  almost monopoly status over how  Canadians access  content through the Internet are not  remitting any of their revenue back to  the publishers who are creating the  value at the end of that chain in France  Australia and elsewhere governments are  taking action Sookie so the digital  Giants are thriving in this crisis says  the French finance minister who is  proposing an EU tax on the one I can  assure you that Minister gable is  working very hard very closely with  allies around the world recently the  Prime Minister did not deem it a  priority but now sounds more committed  we need the media now during a crisis  more than we ever have a close ally of  the Prime Minister on free trade and  president of unifor which represents  some global news employees Jerry Diaz is  critical of Ottawa's foot-dragging on  Internet companies Facebook declined to  comment though some social sites do also  drive consumers to Canadian news sites  but by not paying taxes here or for  content internet Titans are not  recognizing their obligations as this  journalism authority to acknowledge the  fact that they are basically taking away  the product at the same time as our  product our news product is more in  demand than ever  media businesses are facing a deeper  threat to their bottom line than ever  and media companies bottom line is just  one of many the government must deal  with these days  Eric Soren